1:["$","$Ld",null,{"page":{"id":"library--sound-study","kind":"library-article","route":"/roadmap/library/sound-study","sourceUrl":"https://refold.la/roadmap/library/sound-study","sourceHash":"08422869a220e7715a64189696ac94dd62478e697456d5bc1f3c44f13fa052d3","collectedAt":"2026-09-01T23:11:03.810Z","bodyMode":"authorized-full-body","title":{"en":"Sound Study","zh":"语音学习"},"description":{"en":"","zh":""},"segments":[{"id":"segment-0001","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"Sound study means learning about the sounds used in your target language — its phonology. Your target language likely has sounds that don't exist in your native language. Studying them, even briefly, gives your ears a head start in recognizing them during immersion.","zh":"语音学习是指了解目标语言所使用的声音，也就是它的音系。你的目标语言很可能包含母语中不存在的声音。哪怕只进行短暂学习，也能让你的耳朵在沉浸过程中更快地识别这些声音。"}},{"id":"what-to-learn","type":"heading","level":2,"content":{"en":"What to Learn","zh":"要学习什么"}},{"id":"segment-0003","type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"en":"What sounds (phonemes) exist in the language","zh":"这种语言中存在哪些声音（音位）"},{"en":"Which sounds are different from your native language","zh":"哪些声音与母语不同"},{"en":"How those sounds are produced (where your tongue goes, whether your lips round, etc.)","zh":"这些声音是如何发出的（舌头放在哪里、嘴唇是否收圆等）"},{"en":"Common sound changes in connected speech (how sounds shift when words flow together)","zh":"连贯语流中常见的音变（单词连在一起时，声音会如何变化）"},{"en":"The basic stress and intonation patterns","zh":"基本的重音和语调模式"}]},{"id":"segment-0004","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"You don't need to memorize the International Phonetic Alphabet (IPA), but it's a useful tool. When you see a different IPA symbol, it means a different sound — so it helps you notice distinctions you might otherwise miss.","zh":"你不需要记住国际音标（IPA），但它是一种很有用的工具。看到不同的国际音标符号，就表示对应着不同的声音——因此，它能帮助你注意到原本可能忽略的区别。"}},{"id":"how-much-time-to-spend","type":"heading","level":2,"content":{"en":"How Much Time to Spend","zh":"需要投入多少时间"}},{"id":"segment-0006","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"A few sessions of 10-20 minutes is enough to get the basics. You don't need to master phonology — just get enough awareness that you can notice these sounds when they appear in your immersion. The real learning happens through exposure.","zh":"进行几次、每次 10～20 分钟的学习，就足以掌握基础。你不需要精通音系学——只需培养足够的意识，让自己能在沉浸中注意到这些声音。真正的学习会通过接触语言来发生。"}},{"id":"finding-resources","type":"heading","level":2,"content":{"en":"Finding Resources","zh":"寻找学习资源"}},{"id":"segment-0008","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"Look for YouTube videos by experienced teachers of your target language. Many have dedicated pronunciation or sound system videos. Fluent Forever's pronunciation trainers are also well-regarded. The Wikipedia article on your target language's phonology can be surprisingly useful as a reference.","zh":"可以在 YouTube 上寻找由经验丰富的目标语言教师制作的视频。许多教师都有专门讲解发音或语音系统的视频。Fluent Forever 的发音训练工具也广受好评。目标语言音系的维基百科条目作为参考资料，往往也出乎意料地实用。"}},{"id":"tips","type":"heading","level":2,"content":{"en":"Tips","zh":"建议"}},{"id":"segment-0010","type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"en":"Focus on hearing the sounds, not producing them (unless you're in the Speaking phase)","zh":"专注于听辨声音，而不是发出声音（除非你已经进入口语阶段）"},{"en":"Don't trust the writing system — many languages spell things differently from how they sound","zh":"不要完全相信书写系统——许多语言的拼写与实际发音并不一致"},{"en":"If you can't hear the difference between two sounds, that's normal. It takes time and exposure.","zh":"如果你听不出两个声音之间的区别，这很正常。辨别它们需要时间和持续接触。"},{"en":"Come back to sound study at different stages — you'll notice new things each time","zh":"在不同阶段重新进行语音学习——每次回来，你都会注意到新的东西"}]},{"id":"when-to-start","type":"heading","level":2,"content":{"en":"When to Start","zh":"何时开始"}},{"id":"segment-0012","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"Sound study begins in Phase 1A and is revisited in Phase 3B when choosing a dialect.","zh":"语音学习从阶段 1A 开始，并会在阶段 3B 选择方言时再次进行。"}},{"id":"research-and-reasoning","type":"heading","level":2,"content":{"en":"Research and Reasoning","zh":"研究与原理"}},{"id":"segment-0014","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"When you learn your first language as a child, your brain tunes itself to the sounds that matter in that language — and tunes out the ones that don't. By adulthood, your native language acts as a perceptual filter. Best's (1995) Perceptual Assimilation Model (PAM) describes how this works: when an L2 sound is similar to an L1 sound, learners map it onto the familiar category, making it hard to notice the difference. This is why sound study matters — without some explicit awareness of what's different, your brain will quietly ignore distinctions that are meaningful in the target language.","zh":"小时候学习第一语言时，大脑会调整自己，关注该语言中有意义的声音，同时忽略无关的声音。成年后，母语就像一道感知过滤器。Best（1995）的知觉同化模型（PAM）描述了这一机制：当第二语言中的声音与第一语言中的某个声音相似时，学习者会把它归入熟悉的声音类别，因此难以察觉两者的差异。这正是语音学习很重要的原因——如果没有明确意识到差异所在，大脑就会悄悄忽略目标语言中具有意义的声音区别。"}},{"id":"segment-0015","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"The good news is that this filtering is not permanent. Flege and Bohn's (2021) revised Speech Learning Model (SLM-r) proposes that the mechanisms for learning new sound categories remain available across the lifespan. Research on High Variability Phonetic Training has shown that even short perceptual training sessions produce gains in L2 sound discrimination that generalize to new speakers and contexts (Sakai &amp; Moorman, 2018). The goal isn't mastery — it's building enough awareness that your brain knows what to listen for during immersion.","zh":"好消息是，这种过滤并非永久不变。Flege 和 Bohn（2021）修订后的言语学习模型（SLM-r）提出，人一生中始终保有学习新声音类别的机制。有关高变异语音训练的研究表明，即使是短时间的感知训练，也能提高学习者辨别第二语言声音的能力，而且这种提升可以迁移到新的说话者和语境中（Sakai &amp; Moorman，2018）。目标并非精通语音，而是培养足够的意识，让大脑知道在沉浸过程中应该留意什么。"}},{"id":"segment-0016","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"The recommendation to focus on hearing before producing reflects a well-established principle in L2 phonology. Flege's (1995) Speech Learning Model posits that accurate production of an L2 sound cannot occur unless the learner has first formed a perceptual representation of it. While more recent work suggests this relationship is somewhat bidirectional (Flege &amp; Bohn, 2021), the broad consensus remains that attuning your ears first gives production a foundation to build on.","zh":"先专注于听辨、再练习发音的建议，体现了第二语言音系学中一项得到充分认可的原则。Flege（1995）的言语学习模型认为，除非学习者先形成对某个第二语言声音的感知表征，否则无法准确发出这个声音。尽管较新的研究表明这种关系在一定程度上是双向的（Flege &amp; Bohn，2021），但普遍共识仍然是：先训练耳朵，能为之后的发音打下基础。"}}],"cards":[]}}]
